ntdll: what an allocation returned, not only what it was asked.

NtAllocateVirtualMemory TRACEs its arguments on entry, so a VirtualAlloc(NULL,
...) records the address as 0x0 and the address actually chosen is never written
down. The common case is therefore unattributable: the log shows that something
asked for N bytes, and separately that some region exists, and gives no way to
join them.

That cost a day on Resident Evil Requiem. A 240-byte memmove faults 16 bytes
past a three-page allocation at 0x22F40000, in the same place every run, and the
question that decides whose defect it is -- did the guest ask for more than
three pages? -- cannot be answered, because the request that produced
0x22F40000 logged its address as zero.

A source audit of every path from NtAllocateVirtualMemory to the mmap found no
way for this file to commit less than was asked: sizes only round up, the
free-area search skips a range that is too small rather than clamping to it
(virtual.c:2341), and a commit past a view's end returns STATUS_NOT_MAPPED_VIEW
instead of committing part of it. The fault report agrees -- had a larger
request been shorted, view->size would still be the full size and the fault
would have printed 0043's "in view ... committed" WARN rather than the "no view
covers it" ERR it did print. So Wine's bookkeeping believes the allocation is
three pages, and that number is the guest's own rounded request. This trace is
what turns that inference into a record.

On its own channel, for the reason 0043 exists: `virtual` at TRACE is 85% view
dumps and elided three quarters of a session, so a line that answers a question
does not survive in it. `+virtualres` is one line per successful allocation.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -106,6 +106,7 @@
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtual_ranges);
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtstat);
 WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtual_views);
+WINE_DECLARE_DEBUG_CHANNEL(virtualres);
 
 /* Gdb integration, in loader/main.c */
 static struct r_debug *wine_r_debug;
@@ -3725,6 +3726,30 @@
         if (is_builtin && !offset) add_builtin_module( view->base, NULL );
         *addr_ptr = view->base;
         *size_ptr = size;
+
+        /* Vessel: what the allocation *returned*, on a channel of its own.
+         *
+         * NtAllocateVirtualMemory's own TRACE prints its arguments on entry, so
+         * a VirtualAlloc(NULL, ...) -- which is most of them -- records the
+         * address as 0x0 and the address actually chosen is never written down.
+         * That makes the common case unattributable: you can see that something
+         * asked for N bytes, and separately that some region exists, and never
+         * join the two.
+         *
+         * It cost a day on Requiem. A 240-byte memmove faults 16 bytes past a
+         * three-page allocation at 0x22F40000, deterministically, and the one
+         * question that decides whose defect it is -- did the guest ask for more
+         * than three pages? -- could not be answered from the log, because the
+         * request that produced 0x22F40000 logged its address as zero.
+         *
+         * Its own channel rather than `virtual`, for the reason 0043 exists:
+         * `virtual` at TRACE is 85% view dumps and elided three quarters of a
+         * session, so the one line that answers a question does not survive in
+         * it. `+virtualres` is one line per successful allocation and can be
+         * read on its own.
+         */
+        TRACE_(virtualres)( "returned %p size %08lx type %x prot %08x\n",
+                            base, size, type, protect );
         VIRTUAL_DEBUG_DUMP_VIEW( view );
     }
     else delete_view( view );
